import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
---

<ServiceLayout
  title="Estate Planning Before Retirement | Pre-Retirement Checklist | Mesa Estate Planning"
  description="Approaching retirement is the time to refresh your healthcare directive, review beneficiary deeds, plan for long-term care, and optimize retirement account designations. Mesa, AZ."
  heroTitle="You're about to stop earning. Make sure your plan is ready for what comes next."
  heroSubtitle="The transition from accumulating wealth to preserving and distributing it requires a different plan — one built around healthcare decisions, beneficiary coordination, and protecting what you've built from long-term care costs."
  breadcrumbParent="Life Stages"
  breadcrumbParentUrl="/life-stages"
  keywords="estate planning retirement arizona, pre-retirement estate planning, long-term care planning arizona, beneficiary deed arizona, healthcare directive update"
  relatedServices={[
    { title: 'Healthcare Directive', href: '/services/healthcare-directive', desc: 'Update your medical wishes and healthcare agent.' },
    { title: 'Wills & Trusts', href: '/services/wills-and-trusts', desc: 'Trust updates for the distribution phase of life.' },
    { title: 'Estate Tax Planning', href: '/services/estate-tax-planning', desc: 'Minimize taxes on wealth transfers.' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>The years immediately before retirement are a critical planning window. Your <a href="/services/healthcare-directive">healthcare directive</a> needs to reflect your current wishes and name agents who are available and willing. Retirement account beneficiary designations need to be coordinated with your trust. <a href="/glossary#arizona-beneficiary-deed">Arizona beneficiary deeds</a> on real property should be reviewed. And if long-term care is a concern — and for most families it should be — planning now provides options that won't exist once care is needed.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>For most of your working life, estate planning was about protecting your family if something happened to you prematurely. As you approach retirement, the focus shifts. The question is no longer just "what happens if I die?" — it's "what happens as I age?"</p>

    <p>Healthcare decisions become more likely and more consequential. The people you named as agents 20 years ago may have moved, aged themselves, or may no longer be the right choice. Your assets are at their peak value, and the way they're titled, designated, and structured determines how efficiently they transfer — and how much your family pays in taxes, probate fees, and administrative costs.</p>

    <p>If you haven't reviewed your estate plan in the last three to five years, or if it was created for a significantly different life stage, this is the moment to bring it current.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>The Pre-Retirement Estate Planning Checklist</h2>
    <p><strong>Healthcare Directive Refresh.</strong> Review your healthcare agent designation and your treatment preferences. Are the people you named still willing and able to serve? Do your wishes about life-sustaining treatment still reflect your current values? Arizona's healthcare directive statute (A.R.S. § 36-3221) allows you to be as specific or as general as you want — but specificity reduces conflict when decisions have to be made quickly.</p>

    <p><strong>Financial Power of Attorney Update.</strong> The person managing your finances during incapacity should be someone you trust completely, who lives nearby or can travel quickly, and who understands your financial situation. If your current agent is a spouse who may also be aging, naming a successor agent — an adult child, a trusted professional — is essential.</p>

    <p><strong>Retirement Account Beneficiary Review.</strong> IRAs, 401(k)s, 403(b)s, and other qualified plans have beneficiary designations that override your will and trust. The SECURE Act changed the rules for inherited retirement accounts — most non-spouse beneficiaries must now withdraw the entire account within 10 years. Coordinating these designations with your overall plan can save your beneficiaries significant tax liability.</p>

    <p><strong>Arizona Beneficiary Deed Review.</strong> If you have an <a href="/glossary#arizona-beneficiary-deed">Arizona beneficiary deed</a> on your home or other real property, verify that it still names the right people and that it's coordinated with your trust. A beneficiary deed that conflicts with your trust creates confusion and potential legal disputes.</p>

    <p><strong>Long-Term Care Consideration.</strong> The average cost of a private room in an Arizona nursing facility exceeds $9,000 per month. Long-term care insurance, Medicaid planning (within legal and ethical boundaries), and trust structures that preserve assets while qualifying for benefits are all strategies best implemented before care is needed — not after.</p>

    <p><strong>Trust Review and Restatement.</strong> If your revocable trust was drafted more than five years ago, the law may have changed, your assets may have changed, and your wishes may have changed. A trust restatement updates the entire document without creating a new trust — preserving the original date and funding.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Pre-retirement plan reviews and updates are flat-fee, quoted after your <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. If your existing plan is solid and just needs minor updates, the cost reflects that. If a more comprehensive overhaul is needed, we'll explain why and quote accordingly.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          I have a plan from 15 years ago. Does it still work?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>It may be legally valid, but it almost certainly doesn't reflect current law, current tax figures, or your current situation. The SECURE Act alone changed how inherited retirement accounts are taxed. Arizona law has also evolved. A 15-year-old plan should be reviewed by an attorney who can identify gaps and recommend targeted updates.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Should my adult children be involved in my estate planning?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>That's a personal decision, but in most cases, some level of communication is valuable. Your children don't need to know the dollar amounts, but they should know where your documents are, who your agents are, and what your general wishes are. If an adult child is named as trustee, healthcare agent, or power of attorney agent, they should understand what that role involves before they need to act.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What's the difference between a beneficiary deed and a trust for my house?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>An <a href="/glossary#arizona-beneficiary-deed">Arizona beneficiary deed</a> transfers your home directly to a named beneficiary at death, bypassing probate. It's simple and effective for straightforward situations. A trust provides more control — you can add conditions, manage multiple properties, and coordinate the home with your overall distribution plan. For most retirees with a comprehensive estate plan, holding the home in the trust is the better approach.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Is it too late to get long-term care insurance?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>It depends on your age and health. Premiums increase significantly with age, and applicants with pre-existing conditions may be denied coverage. If you're in your late 50s or early 60s and in good health, it may still be a viable option. If traditional long-term care insurance isn't available or affordable, there are hybrid products and planning strategies that can help. We discuss the options during the consultation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
</ServiceLayout>
